<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- One station's place in a running line.

     THIS FILE IS WRITTEN ONCE AND SAYS WHAT A STATION DOES. How many stations
     there are, what each is called, which arm serves it, what it is connected to
     and which sensor tells it a part has arrived are DATA — they arrive as static
     remaps on the `<SubTree>` element that instantiates this tree, generated from
     the L0 topology by `line_tree.hpp`. Nothing below names a station, an asset,
     a frame, a topic or an action. If it ever does, the model has stopped being
     the single source of truth (P1, P5).

     Every name below is a blackboard reference. Neither this file nor the
     coordinator writes an asset name or an action name: they arrive as
     parameters, because names are generated from the L0 model and no asset name
     is written by hand twice (CLAUDE.md §8).

     A subtree's blackboard is its own in BT.CPP v4, so `{workpiece}`, `{token}`
     and `{detected_pose}` below are private to this station by construction.
     "Blackboard used as a global store — untraceable coupling between subtrees"
     is a named L4 failure mode, and this is what keeps it out.

     WHAT IS AND IS NOT L4's. Every motion leaf is an L3 skill called as a ROS 2
     action. Nothing here plans a trajectory or commands a controller — that
     separation is what lets an arm be swapped without touching orchestration
     (P9). The handoff is split the same way (ADR-0024): the four protocol leaves
     below negotiate ownership, and the physical motion is `PlaceAt` — or, for a
     direct arm-to-arm handoff, `TransferTo`, which `line_plan.hpp` currently
     refuses to instantiate because a grasp does not hold an orientation.

     ORDER MATTERS IN TWO PLACES, and both are protocol rather than convenience:

       * `OfferHandoff` → `AwaitHandoffConfirmed` → `PlaceAt` → `CompleteHandoff`.
         Nothing physical happens until both parties have confirmed (rule 2), and
         ownership moves only after the robot has let go (rule 1). A handoff that
         fails anywhere before `CompleteHandoff` leaves the work-piece with this
         station, because nothing touched the registry — which is what makes the
         timeout an outcome rather than an ambiguity (rule 3).

       * `ClaimBufferSlot` before `OfferHandoff`. A station that offered a piece
         it has nowhere to put would be promising room it has not got.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4">

  <BehaviorTree ID="LineStation">
    <Repeat num_cycles="-1">
      <Fallback name="cycle_with_recovery">

        <Sequence name="nominal">

          <!-- Idle: waiting for work, and saying yes to work being offered.
               Both at once, because a station confirms a handoff while it is
               free rather than while it is busy — a station that could only
               confirm between cycles would stall the line behind its own.
               `AcceptOffers` never returns SUCCESS; the Parallel ends when the
               wait does, and halting it is how it stops. -->
          <Parallel name="idle" success_count="1" failure_count="1">
            <AwaitTrigger  station="{station}" topic="{trigger_topic}" state="{trigger_state}"
                           sensed_workpiece="{sensed_workpiece}" />
            <AcceptOffers  station="{station}" />
          </Parallel>

          <SetStationState station="{station}" state="2" />

          <!-- Both frames at once, in a canonical order. Claiming them one at a
               time is the circular wait two stations deadlock on. -->
          <ClaimReach    station="{station}" pick_frame="{pick_frame}"
                         place_frame="{place_frame}" />

          <!-- Confirm the part is still there before an arm is sent for it.

               IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT YAW, and this comment used to say it did:
               "where the part actually is, including its yaw ... the leaf that
               makes the grasp safe against the rotation a previous gripper left
               in it". ADR-0031's correction retracted exactly that sentence.
               Every detector in this cell is a break beam; a through-beam knows
               that something crossed it and nothing about where along itself, let
               alone about its orientation. `Detect` returns an explicitly
               unobserved pose, `PickAt` refuses it through
               `cite_skills::pose_is_observed`, and the pick goes to the station's
               L0 frame — which is the NORMAL path here, not a fallback anyone is
               waiting to replace.

               So what this leaf is for is the other half of its name. The
               station's trigger said a part arrived; this says it is still there.
               An empty result is a disagreement between trigger and detector, and
               `DetectAt` reports it as a failure rather than looking again. -->
          <DetectAt      asset="{asset}" action="{detect_action}" frame="{pick_frame}"
                         pose="{detected_pose}" workpiece="{detected_workpiece}" />

          <TakeCustody   station="{station}" admits_work="{admits_work}"
                         workpiece="{workpiece}" />

          <PickAt        asset="{asset}" action="{pick_action}" frame="{pick_frame}"
                         pose="{detected_pose}" workpiece="{workpiece}" />

          <!-- The piece is off the link it arrived on, so its slot is free for
               the station upstream. Released here rather than at the end of the
               cycle: an upstream station waiting on a full belt should be
               released by the pick, not by this arm finishing its placement. -->
          <ReleaseClaim  resource="{inbound_buffer}" claimant="{workpiece}" />

          <ClaimBufferSlot buffer="{outbound_buffer}" workpiece="{workpiece}" />

          <OfferHandoff  station="{station}" downstream="{downstream}"
                         workpiece="{workpiece}" token="{token}" />
          <AwaitHandoffConfirmed token="{token}" />

          <PlaceAt       asset="{asset}" action="{place_action}" frame="{place_frame}" />

          <CompleteHandoff station="{station}" token="{token}"
                           location="{outbound_location}" />

          <!-- The belt this station picks from runs again (ADR-0032).

               It was stopped when this station's trigger fired, so that the piece
               stood still at the pick point instead of riding past it. The beam
               that fired stands 0.027 m DOWNSTREAM of that point — half a part
               length plus half a beam width, derived in
               `cite_tools.model.resolve.index_offset_m` rather than authored — so
               a part breaks it at the instant its own centre reaches the pick
               point (ADR-0033). There is therefore no travel budget to spend:
               every metre the belt runs after that edge is a metre the part is in
               the wrong place, and at the declared 0.150 m/s it clears its own
               0.050 m length in 0.333 s, against a station cycle of roughly two
               minutes.

               STOPPING IT IS NOT A LEAF, and its absence here is deliberate
               rather than an omission. A leaf acts when this station's cycle
               reaches it; a work-piece reaches the beam whenever it reaches the
               beam, including while this station is still placing the previous
               one. So the stop is bound to the sensor edge itself, in
               `conveyor_index.hpp`, which is where the reasoning is written down.
               ADR-0032 names the two ends separately for this reason: "a
               DetectionEvent transition in, an ADR-0024 protocol leaf out".

               HERE rather than at `PickAt`, which ADR-0032 left open: see
               `ResumeBelt` in `line_nodes.hpp` for the three reasons. Not in the
               recovery branch below either — a station that failed may not have
               picked, and running a belt with a piece still on it puts that piece
               on the floor. -->
          <ResumeBelt    belt="{inbound_belt}" />

          <ReleaseClaim  resource="{pick_frame}"  claimant="{station}" />
          <ReleaseClaim  resource="{place_frame}" claimant="{station}" />

          <MoveToHome    asset="{asset}" action="{move_to_action}" />
          <SetStationState station="{station}" state="0" />

        </Sequence>

        <!-- Recovery is expressed, not implied — and NOTHING MOVES BEFORE THE
             POLICY HAS CHOSEN (ADR-0037).

             `RecoverFromFailure` is the FIRST leaf, and that ordering is the
             decision. It used to be the third, after `MoveToHome`: so a station
             that failed planned and drove a fresh trajectory, unattended, before
             anything had asked whether it was allowed to move at all. Two policy
             rows say plainly that it must not. `SAFETY_BLOCKED`'s own comment
             says L4 must never treat a refusal as a transient; `HARDWARE_FAULT`'s
             says the cell is not in a state to be commanded — after which, in
             that order, the cell was commanded.

             CLEARING THE ARM IS AN ACTION OF THE RETRY PATH. The justification
             that used to stand here — "a station that failed mid-cycle may have
             left the arm somewhere the next attempt would collide with" — is
             sound and was in the wrong place. It argues for clearing the arm
             INSIDE the retry, not before the decision to allow one. If the answer
             is escalate, there is no next attempt to protect. So the comment
             moves with the node rather than being deleted, and a station whose
             classification is ESCALATE or STOP_LINE now performs no motion at
             all: it stops where it is.

             THE ORDER IS NOT WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE, and a reader adding a leaf
             here should know why. `RecoverFromFailure` reads the failing
             `ResultCode` from the blackboard, and every `SkillNode` used to write
             that key on SUCCESS as well as on failure — so the old `MoveToHome`
             overwrote the very code the policy was about to read, the policy saw
             SUCCESS, answered NONE, and the Repeat retried. ESCALATE and
             STOP_LINE were unreachable here. That is fixed at both ends: a
             successful skill no longer writes the key, and `RecoverFromFailure`
             consumes it as it reads. The ordering below is still the right
             ordering; it is no longer the only thing holding.

             `RecoverFromFailure` decides from the structured `ResultCode`, never
             from text. SUCCESS means try again and the Repeat above does exactly
             that; FAILURE means a person is needed, and it propagates to the line
             tree, which halts every other station and so cancels their goals.

             WHAT HAPPENS TO THE STATION'S CLAIMS IS A DECISION, and an earlier
             version of this comment stated the opposite of what the tree does. It
             said `ReleaseStationClaims` "runs on BOTH answers". It cannot: it is
             the second leaf of a `<Sequence>`, `RecoverFromFailure` returns
             FAILURE on ESCALATE and on STOP_LINE, and a Sequence stops there. So
             the claims are released ON THE RETRY PATH AND ON NO OTHER ANSWER, and
             that is the behaviour this file means.

             AN ESCALATING STATION KEEPS ITS CLAIMS. It performs no motion, so it
             is still standing in the frames it reached into and still holds the
             outbound slot for a piece `RecoverFromFailure` deliberately left with
             it. A claim is the line's record of what a station occupies;
             releasing one here would tell the arbiter a frame is free while an
             arm holds a position nothing has established — the same kind of
             statement about the world that ADR-0037 exists to stop being made,
             and the one a `MOTION_INTERRUPTED` abort has just contradicted.

             Starving the neighbour is the CORRECT consequence and not a cost to
             work around. An escalating station stops the line: its FAILURE fails
             the root `Parallel`, and `L4-orchestration.md`'s designed
             `OnFault -> StopAll -> AwaitReset` stops it deliberately. There is no
             station left running to be starved.

             NONE OF THIS IS A PROTECTIVE MEASURE. `resource_arbiter.hpp` says of
             itself that it prevents deadlock and thrash and that relying on it
             for anything else is a defect. What is kept honest here is the
             ALLOCATION RECORD, which is a coordination property.

             The operator reset (ADR-0037 decision 5) does nothing about them, on
             purpose: reset is not start. A station returns to WAITING still
             holding what it held, and the next cycle's `ClaimReach` asks again
             and is answered GRANTED because it is already the holder. Whether an
             operator should be able to release a station's claims separately is
             open, and belongs with the deliberate "clear the arm" action that
             decision also leaves out of scope. -->
        <Sequence name="recover">
          <RecoverFromFailure   station="{station}" workpiece="{workpiece}"
                                token="{token}" />
          <ReleaseStationClaims station="{station}" />

          <!-- The retry path, reached only because the policy chose to retry.
               A station that failed mid-cycle may have left the arm somewhere the
               next attempt would collide with, so the next attempt begins by
               clearing it.

               It only works because a leaf that gives up cancels the goal it
               abandons — see `SkillNode`. Without that, `MoveToHome` here is sent
               to a server still executing the goal that just failed, and the
               skill server rejects it: the recovery branch would fail every time,
               for a reason that appears nowhere in this file. -->
          <MoveToHome           asset="{asset}" action="{move_to_action}" />
        </Sequence>

      </Fallback>
    </Repeat>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
